#: source/language/syntax.md:1
msgid "Language Constructs"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:3
msgid "This section covers syntax and semantics of the Nix language."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:5
msgid "Basic Literals"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:7
msgid "String"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:9
msgid "See [String literals](string-literals.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:11
msgid "Number"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:15
msgid ""
"Numbers, which can be _integers_ (like `123`) or _floating point_ (like "
"`123.43` or `.27e13`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:18
msgid ""
"Integers in the Nix language are 64-bit [two's complement](https://en."
"wikipedia.org/wiki/Two%27s_complement) signed integers, with a range of "
"-9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854775807, inclusive."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:22
msgid ""
"Note that negative numeric literals are actually parsed as unary negation of "
"positive numeric literals. This means that the minimum integer "
"`-9223372036854775808` cannot be written as-is as a literal, since the "
"positive number `9223372036854775808` is one past the maximum range."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:25
msgid ""
"See [arithmetic](./operators.md#arithmetic) and [comparison](./operators."
"md#comparison) operators for semantics."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:30
msgid "Path"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:32
msgid "_Paths_ can be expressed by path literals such as `./builder.sh`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:34
msgid ""
"A path literal must contain at least one slash to be recognised as such. For "
"instance, `builder.sh` is not a path: it's parsed as an expression that "
"selects the attribute `sh` from the variable `builder`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:38
msgid ""
"Path literals are resolved relative to their [base directory](../glossary."
"md#gloss-base-directory). Path literals may also refer to absolute paths by "
"starting with a slash."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:41 source/language/syntax.md:338
msgid "**Note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:43
msgid ""
"Absolute paths make expressions less portable. In the case where a function "
"translates a path literal into an absolute path string for a configuration "
"file, it is recommended to write a string literal instead. This avoids some "
"confusion about whether files at that location will be used during "
"evaluation. It also avoids unintentional situations where some function "
"might try to copy everything at the location into the store."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:48
msgid ""
"If the first component of a path is a `~`, it is interpreted such that the "
"rest of the path were relative to the user's home directory. For example, `~/"
"foo` would be equivalent to `/home/edolstra/foo` for a user whose home "
"directory is `/home/edolstra`. Path literals that start with `~` are not "
"allowed in [pure](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-pure-eval) evaluation."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:52
msgid ""
"Path literals can also include [string interpolation](./string-interpolation."
"md), besides being [interpolated into other expressions](./string-"
"interpolation.md#interpolated-expression)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:57
msgid ""
"At least one slash (`/`) must appear _before_ any interpolated expression "
"for the result to be recognized as a path."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:59
msgid ""
"`a.${foo}/b.${bar}` is a syntactically valid number division operation. `./a."
"${foo}/b.${bar}` is a path."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:62
msgid ""
"[Lookup path](./constructs/lookup-path.md) literals such as `<nixpkgs>` also "
"resolve to path values."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:64
msgid "List"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:66
msgid ""
"Lists are formed by enclosing a whitespace-separated list of values between "
"square brackets. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:69
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"[ 123 ./foo.nix \"abc\" (f { x = y; }) ]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:73
msgid ""
"defines a list of four elements, the last being the result of a call to the "
"function `f`. Note that function calls have to be enclosed in parentheses. "
"If they had been omitted, e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:77
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"[ 123 ./foo.nix \"abc\" f { x = y; } ]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:81
msgid ""
"the result would be a list of five elements, the fourth one being a function "
"and the fifth being a set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:84
msgid "Note that lists are only lazy in values, and they are strict in length."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:86
msgid ""
"Elements in a list can be accessed using [`builtins.elemAt`](./builtins."
"md#builtins-elemAt)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:88
msgid "Attribute Set"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:90
msgid ""
"An attribute set is a collection of name-value-pairs called _attributes_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:92
msgid ""
"Attribute sets are written enclosed in curly brackets (`{ }`). Attribute "
"names and attribute values are separated by an equal sign (`=`). Each value "
"can be an arbitrary expression, terminated by a semicolon (`;`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:96
msgid ""
"An attribute name is a string without context, and is denoted by a [name](./"
"identifiers.md#names) (an [identifier](./identifiers.md#identifiers) or "
"[string literal](string-literals.md))."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:100 source/language/syntax.md:124
#: source/language/syntax.md:131
msgid "**Syntax**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:102
msgid "_attrset_ → `{` { _name_ `=` _expr_ `;` } `}`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:104
msgid ""
"Attributes can appear in any order. An attribute name may only occur once in "
"each attribute set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:107 source/language/syntax.md:137
#: source/language/syntax.md:154 source/language/syntax.md:532
#: source/language/syntax.md:551 source/language/syntax.md:714
#: source/language/syntax.md:727 source/language/syntax.md:742
#: source/language/syntax.md:759
msgid "**Example**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:109
msgid "This defines an attribute set with attributes named:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:110
msgid "`x` with the value `123`, an integer"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:111
msgid "`text` with the value `\"Hello\"`, a string"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:112
msgid ""
"`y` where the value is the result of applying the function `f` to the "
"attribute set `{ bla = 456; }`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:114
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{\n"
"  x = 123;\n"
"  text = \"Hello\";\n"
"  y = f { bla = 456; };\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:122
msgid ""
"Attributes in nested attribute sets can be written using _attribute paths_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:126
msgid "_attrset_ → `{` { _attrpath_ `=` _expr_ `;` } `}`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:133
msgid "_attrpath_ = _name_ { `.` _name_ }"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:152
msgid ""
"Attribute names can also be set implicitly by using the [`inherit` keyword]"
"(#inheriting-attributes)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:162
msgid ""
"Attributes can be accessed with the [`.` operator](./operators.md#attribute-"
"selection)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:164 source/language/syntax.md:174
#: source/language/syntax.md:244 source/language/syntax.md:279
#: source/language/syntax.md:299 source/language/syntax.md:316
#: source/language/syntax.md:344
msgid "Example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:166
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ a = \"Foo\"; b = \"Bar\"; }.a\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:170
msgid "This evaluates to `\"Foo\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:172
msgid ""
"It is possible to provide a default value in an attribute selection using "
"the `or` keyword."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:176
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ a = \"Foo\"; b = \"Bar\"; }.c or \"Xyzzy\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:180
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ a = \"Foo\"; b = \"Bar\"; }.c.d.e.f.g or \"Xyzzy\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:184
msgid ""
"will both evaluate to `\"Xyzzy\"` because there is no `c` attribute in the "
"set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:186
msgid "You can use arbitrary double-quoted strings as attribute names:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:188
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ \"$!@#?\" = 123; }.\"$!@#?\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:192
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let bar = \"bar\"; in\n"
"{ \"foo ${bar}\" = 123; }.\"foo ${bar}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:197 source/language/syntax.md:211
msgid "Both will evaluate to `123`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:199
msgid ""
"Attribute names support [string interpolation](./string-interpolation.md):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:201
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let bar = \"foo\"; in\n"
"{ foo = 123; }.${bar}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:206
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let bar = \"foo\"; in\n"
"{ ${bar} = 123; }.foo\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:213
msgid ""
"In the special case where an attribute name inside of a set declaration "
"evaluates to `null` (which is normally an error, as `null` cannot be coerced "
"to a string), that attribute is simply not added to the set:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:217
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ ${if foo then \"bar\" else null} = true; }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:221
msgid "This will evaluate to `{}` if `foo` evaluates to `false`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:223
msgid ""
"A set that has a \\[`__functor`\\]{#attr-\\_\\_functor} attribute whose "
"value is callable (i.e. is itself a function or a set with a `__functor` "
"attribute whose value is callable) can be applied as if it were a function, "
"with the set itself passed in first , e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:228
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let add = { __functor = self: x: x + self.x; };\n"
"    inc = add // { x = 1; }; # inc is { x = 1; __functor = (...) }\n"
"in inc 1 # equivalent of `add.__functor add 1` i.e. `1 + self.x`\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:234
msgid ""
"evaluates to `2`. This can be used to attach metadata to a function without "
"the caller needing to treat it specially, or to implement a form of object-"
"oriented programming, for example."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:238
msgid "Recursive sets"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:240
msgid ""
"Recursive sets are like normal [attribute sets](./types.md#type-attrs), but "
"the attributes can refer to each other."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:242
msgid "_rec-attrset_ = `rec {` \\[ _name_ `=` _expr_ `;` `]`... `}`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:253
msgid "This evaluates to `123`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:255
msgid ""
"Note that without `rec` the binding `x = y;` would refer to the variable `y` "
"in the surrounding scope, if one exists, and would be invalid if no such "
"variable exists. That is, in a normal (non-recursive) set, attributes are "
"not added to the lexical scope; in a recursive set, they are."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:261
msgid ""
"Recursive sets of course introduce the danger of infinite recursion. For "
"example, the expression"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:271
msgid "will crash with an `infinite recursion encountered` error message."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:273
msgid "Let-expressions"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:275
msgid ""
"A let-expression allows you to define local variables for an expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:277
msgid "_let-in_ = `let` \\[ _identifier_ = _expr_ `;` \\]... `in` _expr_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:281
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  x = \"foo\";\n"
"  y = \"bar\";\n"
"in x + y\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:288
msgid "This evaluates to `\"foobar\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:290
msgid ""
"There is also another, older, syntax for let expressions that should not be "
"used in new code:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:292
msgid ""
"_let_ = `let` `{` _identifier_ = _expr_ `;` \\[ _identifier_ = _expr_ `;"
"`\\]...  `}`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:294
msgid "In this form, the attribute set between the `{` `}` is recursive."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:296
msgid ""
"One of the attributes must have the special name `body`, which is the result "
"of the expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:301
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let {\n"
"  foo = bar;\n"
"  bar = \"baz\";\n"
"  body = foo;\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:309
msgid "This evaluates to \"baz\"."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:311
msgid "Inheriting attributes"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:313
msgid ""
"When defining an [attribute set](./types.md#type-attrs) or in a [let-"
"expression](#let-expressions) it is often convenient to copy variables from "
"the surrounding lexical scope (e.g., when you want to propagate attributes). "
"This can be shortened using the `inherit` keyword."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:326 source/language/syntax.md:379
#: source/language/syntax.md:404 source/language/syntax.md:514
msgid "is equivalent to"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:336
msgid "and both evaluate to `{ x = 123; y = 456; }`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:340
msgid ""
"This works because `x` is added to the lexical scope by the `let` construct."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:342
msgid "It is also possible to inherit attributes from another attribute set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:346
msgid "In this fragment from `all-packages.nix`,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:365
msgid ""
"the set used in the function call to the function defined in `../tools/"
"graphics/graphviz` inherits a number of variables from the surrounding scope "
"(`fetchurl` ... `yacc`), but also inherits `libXaw` (the X Athena Widgets) "
"from the `xorg` set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:370
msgid "Summarizing the fragment"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:388
msgid ""
"when used while defining local variables in a let-expression or while "
"defining a set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:391
msgid ""
"In a `let` expression, `inherit` can be used to selectively bring specific "
"attributes of a set into scope. For example"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:415
msgid "both evaluate to `{ names = [ \"a\" \"b\" ]; }`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:417
msgid "Functions"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:419
msgid "Functions have the following form:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:425
msgid ""
"The pattern specifies what the argument of the function must look like, and "
"binds variables in the body to (parts of) the argument. There are three "
"kinds of patterns:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:429
msgid ""
"If a pattern is a single identifier, then the function matches any argument. "
"Example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:432
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let negate = x: !x;\n"
"    concat = x: y: x + y;\n"
"in if negate true then concat \"foo\" \"bar\" else \"\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:438
msgid ""
"Note that `concat` is a function that takes one argument and returns a "
"function that takes another argument. This allows partial parameterisation "
"(i.e., only filling some of the arguments of a function); e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:443
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"map (concat \"foo\") [ \"bar\" \"bla\" \"abc\" ]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:447
msgid "evaluates to `[ \"foobar\" \"foobla\" \"fooabc\" ]`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:449
msgid ""
"A _set pattern_ of the form `{ name1, name2, …, nameN }` matches a set "
"containing the listed attributes, and binds the values of those attributes "
"to variables in the function body. For example, the function"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:458
msgid ""
"can only be called with a set containing exactly the attributes `x`, `y` and "
"`z`. No other attributes are allowed. If you want to allow additional "
"arguments, you can use an ellipsis (`...`):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:466
msgid ""
"This works on any set that contains at least the three named attributes."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:469
msgid ""
"It is possible to provide _default values_ for attributes, in which case "
"they are allowed to be missing. A default value is specified by writing "
"`name ?  e`, where _e_ is an arbitrary expression. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:474
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ x, y ? \"foo\", z ? \"bar\" }: z + y + x\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:478
msgid ""
"specifies a function that only requires an attribute named `x`, but "
"optionally accepts `y` and `z`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:481
msgid ""
"An `@`\\-pattern provides a means of referring to the whole value being "
"matched:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:488
msgid "but can also be written as:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:494
msgid ""
"Here `args` is bound to the argument _as passed_, which is further matched "
"against the pattern `{ x, y, z, ... }`. The `@`\\-pattern makes mainly sense "
"with an ellipsis(`...`) as you can access attribute names as `a`, using "
"`args.a`, which was given as an additional attribute to the function."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:500
msgid "**Warning**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:502
msgid ""
"`args@` binds the name `args` to the attribute set that is passed to the "
"function. In particular, `args` does _not_ include any default values "
"specified with `?` in the function's set pattern."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:505
msgid "For instance"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:523
msgid "and both expressions will evaluate to:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:529
msgid ""
"All bindings introduced by the function are in scope in the entire function "
"expression; not just in the body. It can therefore be used in default values."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:534
msgid ""
"A parameter (`x`), is used in the default value for another parameter (`y`):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:543 source/language/syntax.md:562
msgid "This evaluates to:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:553
msgid ""
"The binding of an `@` pattern, `args`, is used in the default value for a "
"parameter, `x`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:568
msgid ""
"Note that functions do not have names. If you want to give them a name, you "
"can bind them to an attribute, e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:571
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let concat = { x, y }: x + y;\n"
"in concat { x = \"foo\"; y = \"bar\"; }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:576
msgid "Conditionals"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:578
msgid "Conditionals look like this:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:584
msgid ""
"where _e1_ is an expression that should evaluate to a Boolean value (`true` "
"or `false`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:587
msgid "Assertions"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:589
msgid ""
"Assertions are generally used to check that certain requirements on or "
"between features and dependencies hold. They look like this:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:596
msgid ""
"where _e1_ is an expression that should evaluate to a Boolean value. If it "
"evaluates to `true`, _e2_ is returned; otherwise expression evaluation is "
"aborted and a backtrace is printed."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:600
msgid ""
"Here is a Nix expression for the Subversion package that shows how "
"assertions can be used:."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:603
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ localServer ? false\n"
", httpServer ? false\n"
", sslSupport ? false\n"
", pythonBindings ? false\n"
", javaSwigBindings ? false\n"
", javahlBindings ? false\n"
", stdenv, fetchurl\n"
", openssl ? null, httpd ? null, db4 ? null, expat, swig ? null, j2sdk ? "
"null\n"
"}:\n"
"\n"
"assert localServer -> db4 != null; ①\n"
"assert httpServer -> httpd != null && httpd.expat == expat; ②\n"
"assert sslSupport -> openssl != null && (httpServer -> httpd.openssl == "
"openssl); ③\n"
"assert pythonBindings -> swig != null && swig.pythonSupport;\n"
"assert javaSwigBindings -> swig != null && swig.javaSupport;\n"
"assert javahlBindings -> j2sdk != null;\n"
"\n"
"stdenv.mkDerivation {\n"
"  name = \"subversion-1.1.1\";\n"
"  ...\n"
"  openssl = if sslSupport then openssl else null; ④\n"
"  ...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:629
msgid "The points of interest are:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:631
msgid ""
"This assertion states that if Subversion is to have support for local "
"repositories, then Berkeley DB is needed. So if the Subversion function is "
"called with the `localServer` argument set to `true` but the `db4` argument "
"set to `null`, then the evaluation fails."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:636
msgid ""
"Note that `->` is the [logical implication](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Truth_table#Logical_implication) Boolean operation."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:640
msgid ""
"This is a more subtle condition: if Subversion is built with Apache "
"(`httpServer`) support, then the Expat library (an XML library) used by "
"Subversion should be same as the one used by Apache. This is because in this "
"configuration Subversion code ends up being linked with Apache code, and if "
"the Expat libraries do not match, a build- or runtime link error or "
"incompatibility might occur."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:647
msgid ""
"This assertion says that in order for Subversion to have SSL support (so "
"that it can access `https` URLs), an OpenSSL library must be passed. "
"Additionally, it says that _if_ Apache support is enabled, then Apache's "
"OpenSSL should match Subversion's. (Note that if Apache support is not "
"enabled, we don't care about Apache's OpenSSL.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:654
msgid ""
"The conditional here is not really related to assertions, but is worth "
"pointing out: it ensures that if SSL support is disabled, then the "
"Subversion derivation is not dependent on OpenSSL, even if a non-`null` "
"value was passed. This prevents an unnecessary rebuild of Subversion if "
"OpenSSL changes."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:660
msgid "With-expressions"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:662
msgid "A _with-expression_,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:668
msgid ""
"introduces the set _e1_ into the lexical scope of the expression _e2_. For "
"instance,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:671
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let as = { x = \"foo\"; y = \"bar\"; };\n"
"in with as; x + y\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:676
msgid ""
"evaluates to `\"foobar\"` since the `with` adds the `x` and `y` attributes "
"of `as` to the lexical scope in the expression `x + y`. The most common use "
"of `with` is in conjunction with the `import` function. E.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:684
msgid ""
"makes all attributes defined in the file `definitions.nix` available as if "
"they were defined locally in a `let`\\-expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:687
msgid ""
"The bindings introduced by `with` do not shadow bindings introduced by other "
"means, e.g."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:694
msgid "establishes the same scope as"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:700
msgid "Variables coming from outer `with` expressions _are_ shadowed:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:702
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"with { a = \"outer\"; };\n"
"with { a = \"inner\"; };\n"
"a\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:708
msgid "Does evaluate to `\"inner\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:710
msgid "Comments"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:712
msgid "Inline comments start with `#` and run until the end of the line."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:725
msgid ""
"Block comments start with `/*` and run until the next occurrence of `*/`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:729
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"/*\n"
"Block comments\n"
"can span multiple lines.\n"
"*/ \"hello\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:736
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"\"hello\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:740
msgid "This means that block comments cannot be nested."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:757
msgid ""
"Consider escaping nested comments and unescaping them in post-processing."
msgstr ""
